  "id": "cururu_siriri_cuiabano",
  "name": "Cururu and siriri (Cuiabá viola-de-cocho song-dance)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Song-dance traditions of the riverside communities of the Baixada Cuiabana in Mato Grosso, both played on the viola de cocho, a short lute carved from a single log, whose craft IPHAN has registered as Brazilian intangible heritage. Cururu is a men's circle of sung verses for saints' feasts, accompanied by viola de cocho and ganzá, here a notched bamboo scraper; siriri is a festive couple and line dance that adds the mocho, a leather-covered wooden stool struck as a drum. The reco-reco card stands in for the ganzá scraper and a generic membrane-drum card for the mocho.",
  "recipe": "classic modern cururu and siriri, functional Song, Brazilian song branch. viola de cocho, bamboo reco reco, membrane drum pulse resonant acoustic membrane drum. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
